Oh, is it nice?

00:10:49.554 --> 00:10:50.536
Haven't looked at it yet.

00:10:50.536 --> 00:10:51.652
Okay.

00:10:51.652 --> 00:10:53.417
I'm sure it's nice, nice enough.

00:10:53.417 --> 00:10:54.775
We get there,

00:10:54.775 --> 00:10:57.636
this time of year and
especially in that valley,

00:10:57.636 --> 00:10:59.544
the sunset's quick,

00:10:59.544 --> 00:11:04.544
it's like 4.30 and the sun
goes over the mountain ridge

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:07.372
and it gets really dark really quick.

00:11:07.372 --> 00:11:08.360
Yeah.
But we had

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:09.247
kind of beat the sunset

00:11:09.247 --> 00:11:11.865
to get to the first
thing we wanted to see,

00:11:11.865 --> 00:11:15.540
which was some sand dunes in the middle.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:18.207
It's just like sand dunes,

00:11:18.207 --> 00:11:19.832
I don't know how else to describe it,

00:11:19.832 --> 00:11:22.601
there's a really tall sand
dune at the northern end,

00:11:22.601 --> 00:11:23.658
but we're right in the middle,

00:11:23.658 --> 00:11:27.800
coming in through Stovepipe Wells.

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:32.102
Yeah, these are the sand
dunes that are easy to get to

00:11:32.102 --> 00:11:35.539
and it shows, there was a lot
of people on these sand dunes.

00:11:35.539 --> 00:11:37.230
So it's like you're in
the middle of the desert

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:41.058
and in this giant valley,
and we're talking sand dunes

00:11:41.058 --> 00:11:43.846
that are 150/200 feet high,

00:11:43.846 --> 00:11:47.573
maybe some of the biggest ones.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:51.869
There's definitely bigger in other places.

00:11:51.869 --> 00:11:54.836
The Eureka Dunes are like 700 feet tall,

00:11:54.836 --> 00:11:55.970
is what I've heard.

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:56.813
Crazy.
Can you imagine that?

00:11:56.813 --> 00:11:58.542
'Cause these are giant

00:11:58.542 --> 00:12:00.589
and it really is that kind of thing

00:12:00.589 --> 00:12:01.822
that I've only seen in movies

00:12:01.822 --> 00:12:04.889
where somebody'll be in the Sahara

00:12:04.889 --> 00:12:08.480
and it's just like they're
walking up the ridge

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:10.974
of one of those giant sand
dunes, through the desert.

00:12:10.974 --> 00:12:11.950
Yeah.

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:13.610
Well, there's that, that exists here.

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.430
And the boys loved it,

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:17.744
that was a really good
call to go there first

00:12:17.744 --> 00:12:20.154
because we were walking
out on these sand dunes

00:12:20.154 --> 00:12:23.953
and it's really exhausting
walking up these sand dunes,

00:12:23.953 --> 00:12:25.137
but everybody had a ball

00:12:25.137 --> 00:12:27.913
and, you know, you really don't know.

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:33.660
I feel like it was my mission

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:36.459
for my kids to have a good experience.

00:12:36.459 --> 00:12:39.165
I'm building a case for van life,

00:12:39.165 --> 00:12:41.997
or at least being an outdoorsy person.

00:12:41.997 --> 00:12:46.087
And I will say that both of our teenagers

00:12:46.087 --> 00:12:51.087
expressed some teenage-ness
about going, before the trip.

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:53.443
Before the trip.

00:12:53.443 --> 00:12:55.844
Thinking that, going to the desert,

00:12:55.844 --> 00:12:58.917
I don't really understand
why you want me to do this

00:12:58.917 --> 00:13:00.980
and is it really gonna be fun?

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:01.890
But you know what, to their credit,

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:04.000
they brought positive attitude.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:04.833
They had a great attitude
right from the beginning.

00:13:04.833 --> 00:13:07.058
And then when we got on those dunes,

00:13:07.058 --> 00:13:09.260
before we know it, Shepherd,

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:10.761
we get to the highest dune

00:13:10.761 --> 00:13:15.231
that we, realistically,
could walk to and walk up,

00:13:15.231 --> 00:13:19.470
which was one of the highest
ones in that set of dunes

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:21.620
and before I know it, I look over there

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:25.151
and Shepherd is halfway down
the dune doing a log roll

00:13:25.151 --> 00:13:28.558
and just plumes of dust, just coming up.

00:13:28.558 --> 00:13:29.741
I knew this was going to happen

00:13:29.741 --> 00:13:30.894
because I know Shepherd,

00:13:30.894 --> 00:13:33.845
what I didn't know was
that it would inspire Lando

00:13:33.845 --> 00:13:35.833
to, ultimately, try the same thing.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:40.975
He's not a roll down first,

00:13:40.975 --> 00:13:44.410
figure out if it's good idea,
second, kind of a person.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:45.243
Right.

00:13:45.243 --> 00:13:48.535
And, I guess, he observed Shepherd.

00:13:48.535 --> 00:13:51.917
But, you know, it warms
your heart as a dad

00:13:51.917 --> 00:13:54.407
seeing your kids barrel rolling

00:13:54.407 --> 00:13:55.744
and all four of them were doing it

00:13:55.744 --> 00:13:58.937
down this sandy slope.

00:13:58.937 --> 00:14:00.297
But not the dads.

00:14:00.297 --> 00:14:01.945
Not the dads.
Not the two dads.

00:14:01.945 --> 00:14:04.214
I'm thinking about my shoulders,

00:14:04.214 --> 00:14:06.513
this broad shouldered man

00:14:06.513 --> 00:14:08.944
could just crumple up in an instant.

00:14:08.944 --> 00:14:12.471
I just took photos and videos,
I was a cinematographer,

00:14:12.471 --> 00:14:14.470
I was not a roller.

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:15.303
It was a cool start

00:14:15.303 --> 00:14:18.907
and then once we were alarmed

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:22.038
with how quickly it was
getting really dark,

00:14:22.038 --> 00:14:27.038
we found the first place that felt,

00:14:27.239 --> 00:14:32.239
it felt like it could
be, almost, off-road,

00:14:32.972 --> 00:14:35.400
you need a high clearance vehicle

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:38.175
to get to a place where
it would feel isolated

00:14:38.175 --> 00:14:39.919
and we could find a campsite,

00:14:39.919 --> 00:14:43.437
actually, not a campsite, but just a spot.

00:14:43.437 --> 00:14:44.946
Dispersed camping.

00:14:44.946 --> 00:14:45.820
Dispersed camping,

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:47.588
once you get out a mile
off the main roads,

00:14:47.588 --> 00:14:50.271
you're allowed to camp anywhere you want

00:14:50.271 --> 00:14:53.575
in designated swathes of land.

00:14:53.575 --> 00:14:58.273
And there's a beauty to the desert,

00:15:00.533 --> 00:15:03.348
there's a mindset to appreciating it

00:15:03.348 --> 00:15:05.702
but, as we were driving out there,

00:15:05.702 --> 00:15:08.801
and I had the youngest
boys in the van with me,

00:15:08.801 --> 00:15:10.587
and I'm just like

00:15:10.587 --> 00:15:13.090
this is what this van
was made to do, boys.

00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:15.051
We're rollicking over some rocks

00:15:15.051 --> 00:15:19.252
and going through these washes

00:15:19.252 --> 00:15:22.253
with sheer walls on either side.

00:15:22.253 --> 00:15:24.035
There was some slight canyons.

00:15:24.035 --> 00:15:26.650
Yeah, and we were going through that.

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:30.036
And I was like, when I think

00:15:30.036 --> 00:15:34.103
about what I wanted this
trip to be, this is it,

00:15:34.103 --> 00:15:35.986
we're gonna find a good spot.

00:15:35.986 --> 00:15:37.130
Death Valley,

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:39.588
which now me and you
have been to three times,

00:15:39.588 --> 00:15:42.303
Death Valley has it all.

00:15:43.675 --> 00:15:46.959
Well it doesn't have trees,
it doesn't have water,

00:15:46.959 --> 00:15:49.098
it doesn't have shade.

00:15:49.098 --> 00:15:51.355
It has everything that you
would expect in Death Valley

00:15:51.355 --> 00:15:53.706
but, just so you understand

00:15:53.706 --> 00:15:56.593
if you've never been to
Death Valley National Park,

00:15:56.593 --> 00:16:01.201
if you are just picturing
a giant empty desert,

00:16:01.201 --> 00:16:03.756
well, yes, that is part of it,

00:16:03.756 --> 00:16:08.085
but, along the sides of the desert,

00:16:08.085 --> 00:16:13.085
there are so many different
geological formations

00:16:15.027 --> 00:16:17.003
and canyons,

00:16:18.577 --> 00:16:23.577
the kids were immediately into
how cool the geography was,

00:16:24.658 --> 00:16:25.510
right.

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:26.530
Yeah, it's otherworldly.
When you're driving

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:27.722
through this canyon.

00:16:27.722 --> 00:16:32.100
It feels like something
off of a movie set,

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:34.057
you're on a foreign world.

00:16:34.057 --> 00:16:36.647
And if you just have a regular old car,

00:16:36.647 --> 00:16:39.362
a Sedan or whatever, you can
experience a lot of this.

00:16:39.362 --> 00:16:44.362
But if you really wanna get
away from the touristy spots,

00:16:45.483 --> 00:16:47.440
you need a vehicle that can get there.

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:48.524
Now, we had been a couple of times before,

00:16:48.524 --> 00:16:51.837
and, actually, we chronicled
at least one of those trips

00:16:51.837 --> 00:16:52.840
with our friend Nick

00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:55.647
and the other people that
he does overlanding with

00:16:55.647 --> 00:16:56.480
and off-roading with.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:59.690
Yes, when we took the
showers naked in the.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:01.717
Hot springs.
Hot springs, yeah.

00:17:01.717 --> 00:17:03.735
So, yeah,

00:17:03.735 --> 00:17:08.356
we've done the extreme,
technical off-roading thing

00:17:08.356 --> 00:17:11.530
in the FJ, you and I both in that.

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:13.217
With the van and the kids

00:17:13.217 --> 00:17:17.964
and not a lot of confidence
to be able to fix a problem

00:17:17.964 --> 00:17:19.572
in isolated locations,

00:17:19.572 --> 00:17:22.770
like any mechanical or technical issues.

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:26.810
I don't even like to think
about something going wrong

00:17:26.810 --> 00:17:28.928
because I just wanna be in a place

00:17:28.928 --> 00:17:30.074
where, if something goes wrong,

00:17:30.074 --> 00:17:33.747
I can just say it's not up to us.

00:17:33.747 --> 00:17:34.988
Yeah.

00:17:34.988 --> 00:17:38.545
And the van and the rental agreement

00:17:38.545 --> 00:17:39.955
would certainly not allow
us to do what we did before.

00:17:39.955 --> 00:17:41.528
And you wanna minimize,

00:17:41.528 --> 00:17:46.157
you're managing the expectations
of children and teenagers.

00:17:46.157 --> 00:17:47.606
And they don't like to
just stay in the car.

00:17:47.606 --> 00:17:48.439
You don't wanna be in the car too much.

00:17:48.439 --> 00:17:50.830
They wanna get out and do stuff,

00:17:50.830 --> 00:17:52.035
but I will say

00:17:52.035 --> 00:17:53.791
just because you don't like to think

00:17:53.791 --> 00:17:55.388
about something going really wrong,

00:17:55.388 --> 00:17:57.940
doesn't mean it won't, because it did.

00:17:57.940 --> 00:17:58.773
Yes.

00:17:58.773 --> 00:17:59.689
And we'll talk about that.

00:17:59.689 --> 00:18:01.646
We'll get into that and more in a second,

00:18:01.646 --> 00:18:06.646
but first, listen, we've got
the all new Good Mythical mugs,

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:11.690
fresh for season 19 of GMM.

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:12.683
You might say,

00:18:14.054 --> 00:18:16.680
you might even call
this an old school mug,

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.770
black mug, nice GMM,

00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:19.900
but then you're like, nope, nope, nope,

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:23.580
on the other side, you've got
the "Good Mythical Morning"

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:24.413
with Rhett and Link logo,

00:18:24.413 --> 00:18:27.050
so you've got double the logos.

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:27.883
But there's more.

00:18:27.883 --> 00:18:30.053
And a retro styling.

00:18:30.053 --> 00:18:32.925
But there's more, I can't
currently reach it, so.

00:18:32.925 --> 00:18:34.854
Let me do the honors.

00:18:34.854 --> 00:18:38.905
When you fill this thing
up with hot liquids,

00:18:38.905 --> 00:18:40.390
all of a sudden.

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:41.960
It springs to life like
the desert at night.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:46.960
You get the cockatrice in the night sky

00:18:47.116 --> 00:18:51.347
blowing a fiery "Good Mythical
Morning" with Rhett and Link,

00:18:51.347 --> 00:18:53.953
basically inspired by.

00:18:54.919 --> 00:18:56.720
The intro.
The new intro.

00:18:56.720 --> 00:19:01.683
Yeah, so the whole
thing with heated liquid

00:19:03.290 --> 00:19:05.916
reveals a night sky

00:19:05.916 --> 00:19:08.890
and a cockatrice blowing the flame.

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:09.990
And then when it cools down.

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:10.823
It goes away again.

00:19:10.823 --> 00:19:12.470
It goes away and it's black again.

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:17.134
Color changing, scene
inducing, heat activated mugs,

00:19:17.134 --> 00:19:19.748
we keep upping the ante,
that's what we keep doing.

00:19:19.748 --> 00:19:21.038
What's next?

00:19:21.038 --> 00:19:24.707
I don't know, a conscious cup.

00:19:24.707 --> 00:19:25.540
There it is.

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:26.770
Sounds like something

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:29.087
that's made out of recycled materials.

00:19:29.087 --> 00:19:30.977
No, this is a cup that thinks for you.

00:19:30.977 --> 00:19:32.530
Yeah, that's 2022.

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:34.365
I've been letting my cups
think for me for years,

00:19:34.365 --> 00:19:37.510
it just hadn't amounted to
much, see where that's left me.

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:39.150
Mythical.com.
Mythical.com.

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:41.330
Whoa.
Wow, 2021, man,

00:19:41.330 --> 00:19:43.330
we are in sync.
We are in sync, yeah.

00:19:44.953 --> 00:19:46.933
So that first night,

00:19:47.781 --> 00:19:51.877
the older boys had to camp in a tent

00:19:51.877 --> 00:19:54.376
and then me and Lando were
gonna sleep in the van,

00:19:54.376 --> 00:19:56.184
and then you and Shepherd were gonna sleep

00:19:56.184 --> 00:19:59.619
on top of the FJ in that
fancy rooftop setting.

00:19:59.619 --> 00:20:01.319
And you might ask yourself,

00:20:01.319 --> 00:20:03.706
well, why'd you make the
older boys sleep in a tent?

00:20:03.706 --> 00:20:07.930
Well, technically the
rooftop tent that I got

00:20:07.930 --> 00:20:09.897
is a three man tent,

00:20:09.897 --> 00:20:13.786
but if you are a experienced camper,

00:20:13.786 --> 00:20:16.295
you know that three
person means two person.

00:20:16.295 --> 00:20:17.202
It does, doesn't it?

00:20:17.202 --> 00:20:19.161
A three person tent is
comfortable for two people.

00:20:19.161 --> 00:20:20.120
Don't fall for it.

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:23.961
And when one of the persons
is as big as my person,

00:20:23.961 --> 00:20:27.483
even if the other person is
as small as Shepherd's person,

00:20:28.764 --> 00:20:30.983
Shepherd and I were basically
right next to each other,

00:20:30.983 --> 00:20:33.168
could not have comfortably
gotten another being

00:20:33.168 --> 00:20:35.111
in there with us, anyway.

00:20:35.111 --> 00:20:38.844
And you technically could've
gotten somebody into the van,

00:20:38.844 --> 00:20:41.297
but it would've required
moving a bunch of stuff out.

00:20:41.297 --> 00:20:44.396
Yeah, it would be underneath the bed

00:20:44.396 --> 00:20:46.770
and a bunk bed situation.

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:47.937
And we also thought this
was a good opportunity

00:20:47.937 --> 00:20:49.736
for our teenage sons

00:20:49.736 --> 00:20:52.964
to learn how to set a fricking
tent up and break it down.

00:20:52.964 --> 00:20:53.905
You know, what they did it.

00:20:53.905 --> 00:20:56.296
They did.

00:20:56.296 --> 00:21:01.193
I gotta give 'em credit,
man, just positive attitudes.

00:21:02.525 --> 00:21:04.781
If it was just me and my kids,

00:21:04.781 --> 00:21:06.549
it would have been a lot more challenging.

00:21:06.549 --> 00:21:07.382
Yeah.

00:21:07.382 --> 00:21:09.150
And I have to think the
same, the other way, right,

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:11.440
so it was nice that they had compadres.

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:14.387
You're less likely to say
and do embarrassing things

00:21:14.387 --> 00:21:16.560
and lose your cool,

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:18.372
both ways, both the kids and the parents,

00:21:18.372 --> 00:21:20.120
when you are co-mingled.

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:21.018
The thing that I learned the first night,

00:21:21.018 --> 00:21:23.518
it hit me like a ton of
bricks and we talked about it,

00:21:23.518 --> 00:21:28.147
is that my previous camping
experience with Christy,

00:21:28.147 --> 00:21:30.299
the first time we had ever
gone on a camping excursion

00:21:30.299 --> 00:21:32.907
just the two of us and,
of course, you were alone,

00:21:32.907 --> 00:21:35.960
both of those, which are
different than each other,

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:37.237
are still starkly different

00:21:37.237 --> 00:21:40.828
than going camping with multiple children.

00:21:40.828 --> 00:21:44.103
Because the amount of time

00:21:44.103 --> 00:21:46.541
devoted to getting everything right,

00:21:46.541 --> 00:21:48.673
that includes getting
everything right for them,

00:21:48.673 --> 00:21:51.406
we barely had time to sit down.

00:21:51.406 --> 00:21:53.219
We were scrambling to make dinner

00:21:53.219 --> 00:21:56.952
and you got me over here cooking burgers,

00:21:56.952 --> 00:21:59.567
while you're cooking mac
and cheese and other stuff

00:21:59.567 --> 00:22:00.542
on your setup.

00:22:00.542 --> 00:22:03.585
And cooking is a relative term.

00:22:03.585 --> 00:22:08.156
Yeah, I kinda got steaks
and burgers confused,

00:22:08.156 --> 00:22:13.156
and I got in my mind that
if I overcook the burgers,

00:22:14.158 --> 00:22:15.905
it's kinda like a steak,

00:22:15.905 --> 00:22:17.654
if you overcook the steak,
they're gonna complain,

00:22:17.654 --> 00:22:19.971
you don't want it well done.

00:22:19.971 --> 00:22:21.398
But you do want ground beef.

00:22:21.398 --> 00:22:22.520
Yeah.

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:23.608
Unless it's been pasteurized.

00:22:23.608 --> 00:22:26.041
It was kinda dark, but
everyone did seem to notice

00:22:26.041 --> 00:22:27.640
that the burgers weren't
cooked all the way through.

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:29.309
It was more of seared tartare.

00:22:29.309 --> 00:22:30.272
Oh gosh.

00:22:30.272 --> 00:22:31.571
Which I didn't mind.

00:22:31.571 --> 00:22:35.333
I couldn't finish it, Lando was horrified.

00:22:36.679 --> 00:22:38.320
So I was like, okay,

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:40.620
but, you know what, I
played to my strengths

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.820
and when we were done eating,

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:47.197
I said I'll focus on doing
some dishes and cleaning up,

00:22:47.197 --> 00:22:52.197
and I'm back in my zone,
in the cleaning zone.

00:22:52.750 --> 00:22:54.100
There was a lot of cleaning going on.

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:55.963
You were cleaning too.

00:22:58.272 --> 00:23:01.210
Of course it's been dark for hours,

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:02.043
after we're done eating

00:23:02.043 --> 00:23:05.530
and the older boys said,
we're just gonna go off

00:23:05.530 --> 00:23:06.363
and explore,

00:23:06.363 --> 00:23:08.017
I thought that was a good idea.

00:23:08.017 --> 00:23:10.743
So Locke and Lincoln went off,

00:23:10.743 --> 00:23:14.410
they just walked down the way that we came

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:18.169
and there is nothing,
no other civilization

00:23:18.169 --> 00:23:23.050
or no other sign of human
activity within sight.

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:24.641
Really activity of any kind.

00:23:24.641 --> 00:23:25.864
Of any kind.

00:23:25.864 --> 00:23:29.558
There are some plants, not many, but some.

00:23:29.558 --> 00:23:34.558
And so they walk off and
then I'm doing the dishes,

00:23:34.746 --> 00:23:36.749
and I'm trying to remember what happened

00:23:36.749 --> 00:23:38.170
if you told me, yeah.

00:23:38.170 --> 00:23:40.417
I knew as soon as they
left what my my plan was.

00:23:40.417 --> 00:23:42.697
So I come out from finishing the dishes

00:23:42.697 --> 00:23:44.398
and the younger boys
were sitting by the fire,

00:23:44.398 --> 00:23:45.932
and, of course, they've got screens out

00:23:45.932 --> 00:23:47.832
I'm like, boys, put your screens away.

00:23:47.832 --> 00:23:50.666
And I was like, I'm gonna
hang out with you guys

00:23:50.666 --> 00:23:51.909
because you said.

00:23:51.909 --> 00:23:54.609
I'm gonna go scare Locke and Lincoln.

00:23:54.609 --> 00:23:56.667
I'm gonna carry on the
McLaughlin tradition

00:23:56.667 --> 00:24:01.667
of trying to give your fellow
campers a heart attack.

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:07.175
Shepherd and Lando's eyes
got as big as saucers

00:24:07.175 --> 00:24:09.371
and you walked off into the darkness.

00:24:09.371 --> 00:24:10.973
And then I'm left,

00:24:11.854 --> 00:24:13.884
maybe I'll tell my
perspective of this story

00:24:13.884 --> 00:24:16.299
and then you can tell yours.

00:24:16.299 --> 00:24:18.860
So I'm sitting there at the campfire

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:22.243
talking to Shepherd and Lando,

00:24:22.243 --> 00:24:25.129
and I ended up talking about,

00:24:25.129 --> 00:24:27.090
I don't know how we got on it,

00:24:27.090 --> 00:24:28.670
but I started talking to them about,

00:24:28.670 --> 00:24:29.632
I think it's 'cause you're out camping

00:24:29.632 --> 00:24:34.420
and there's a spirit of
independence to camping.

00:24:34.420 --> 00:24:37.737
And I started talking about our friend Ben

00:24:37.737 --> 00:24:41.226
and his adventurous spirit,

00:24:41.226 --> 00:24:43.984
and the things that he
would do in the woods

00:24:43.984 --> 00:24:45.132
and out in nature,

00:24:45.132 --> 00:24:48.711
and how he fostered, the stuff
that we've shared with you

00:24:48.711 --> 00:24:50.910
last year on the podcast.

00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:53.280
I was just kinda telling them stories

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:57.335
of all the things that he would do

00:24:57.335 --> 00:25:00.890
and inspire us to do, out
in the woods and on canoes,

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:05.210
and how his parents,

00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:07.158
when they were not much older than him,

00:25:07.158 --> 00:25:10.197
would take him and his canoe
and drive to Lillington

00:25:10.197 --> 00:25:11.030
and just drop him off.

00:25:11.030 --> 00:25:14.096
Not much older than Shepherd
and Lando, at the time.

00:25:14.096 --> 00:25:14.967
Yeah.

00:25:14.967 --> 00:25:16.500
You said not much older than him,

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:17.996
like his parents weren't
that much older than him.

00:25:17.996 --> 00:25:20.446
No, I said not much older than them.

00:25:20.446 --> 00:25:21.279
Yeah.

00:25:21.279 --> 00:25:22.919
Lando and Shepherd.

00:25:22.919 --> 00:25:25.344
So I was like, can you imagine me and mom

00:25:25.344 --> 00:25:30.030
driving you guys, with a
canoe, to the next town over

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:32.004
and then you're just gonna
canoe down the river,

00:25:32.004 --> 00:25:36.165
and then half a day later,
we're gonna pick you back up.

00:25:36.165 --> 00:25:38.830
And they're super into it,

00:25:38.830 --> 00:25:41.060
and I'm like, yeah, this is
what camping's all about,

00:25:41.060 --> 00:25:42.130
you got a fire,

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:45.722
you got these kids who are
just expanding their horizons

00:25:45.722 --> 00:25:47.937
and forgetting about their screens.

00:25:47.937 --> 00:25:50.232
And then, all of a sudden,

00:25:50.232 --> 00:25:55.232
I just heard this blood curdling scream

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:58.291
and kerfuffle in the way distance,

00:25:58.291 --> 00:26:00.886
and I'm like, Shepherd, that's your dad,

00:26:00.886 --> 00:26:02.837
I think he found the older boys.

00:26:02.837 --> 00:26:04.815
And then we just waited
for you to come back

00:26:04.815 --> 00:26:06.692
and hear the story of what had happened.

00:26:06.692 --> 00:26:09.677
Well, I think I've
talked about this before,

00:26:09.677 --> 00:26:11.027
I have a thing for scaring people,

00:26:11.027 --> 00:26:12.526
it's one of my favorite things

00:26:12.526 --> 00:26:16.520
and I've done it all my life,
and it's a family tradition,

00:26:16.520 --> 00:26:18.020
my dad did it to me.

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:22.110
And so as soon as they left and
they had their headlamps on,

00:26:22.110 --> 00:26:25.368
and we're kind of in this wash,

00:26:25.368 --> 00:26:28.813
so it's, essentially, sort
of like a shallow canyon,

00:26:30.801 --> 00:26:33.162
they can go this way or
they can go that way,

00:26:33.162 --> 00:26:35.250
there's not that many places they can go

00:26:35.250 --> 00:26:37.955
and you can see where they're
at because it's so dark.

00:26:37.955 --> 00:26:40.081
And so I could see that they
were a long distance away,

00:26:40.081 --> 00:26:42.933
'cause I could see their
lights, 'cause it's so flat.

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:47.010
And there was a little bit of moonlight,

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:47.843
there was enough moonlight

00:26:47.843 --> 00:26:51.020
for me to walk out there without a light

00:26:51.020 --> 00:26:52.793
and my eyes adjusted to the desert,

00:26:52.793 --> 00:26:54.434
so I could kind of see where I was going

00:26:54.434 --> 00:26:56.495
without being in too much danger.

00:26:56.495 --> 00:27:00.493
So I was like, okay, I'm
gonna walk down towards them,

00:27:02.199 --> 00:27:03.566
I could see them in the distance,

00:27:03.566 --> 00:27:04.816
oh, they've turned back around

00:27:04.816 --> 00:27:06.434
and now they're walking
back towards the camp site,

00:27:06.434 --> 00:27:07.267
but they're still

00:27:07.267 --> 00:27:10.283
many, many hundreds of yards
away from the campsite.

00:27:10.283 --> 00:27:13.800
So I'm like, all right,
what am I gonna do?

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:17.840
I'm gonna get on the side of this path,

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:21.190
behind this bush, and
wait for them to come by

00:27:21.190 --> 00:27:22.883
and scare the crap out of them.

00:27:22.883 --> 00:27:24.826
But then I was like, man,

00:27:24.826 --> 00:27:29.301
the desert plants are so sparse and small,

00:27:29.301 --> 00:27:33.232
and I'm like, if they're
shining their headlamp

00:27:33.232 --> 00:27:34.410
and they see me

00:27:34.410 --> 00:27:37.091
behind this very sparse
cactus bush kind of thing,

00:27:37.091 --> 00:27:38.458
the whole thing is ruined.

00:27:38.458 --> 00:27:42.363
So I was like, I'm gonna
move further off of the road,

00:27:43.382 --> 00:27:46.617
get down on my belly behind
this little embankment,

00:27:46.617 --> 00:27:51.360
and let them pass and
then come up behind them.

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:55.393
Because not only is that,
you know, they can't see me,

00:27:55.393 --> 00:27:57.580
but it will scare them so much

00:27:57.580 --> 00:28:00.074
to have something come from behind.

00:28:00.074 --> 00:28:00.907
The unknown.

00:28:00.907 --> 00:28:04.192
The unknown that they've just
been in versus the campsite,

00:28:04.192 --> 00:28:08.490
it makes sense that somebody
we know might be on that side.

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:13.490
So they come, they're walking,
they walk by me and then.

00:28:14.741 --> 00:28:16.370
Didn't you hear Locke say something?

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:17.454
Well, here's the deal,

00:28:17.454 --> 00:28:21.632
I had to stand up and start
walking towards the road

00:28:21.632 --> 00:28:23.100
and as I stood up.

00:28:23.100 --> 00:28:24.130
After they passed?

00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:24.963
After they passed,

00:28:24.963 --> 00:28:27.354
as I stood up and started
taking a couple of steps,

00:28:27.354 --> 00:28:29.698
they heard something

00:28:29.698 --> 00:28:32.923
and then later we find out

00:28:32.923 --> 00:28:34.700
Locke was like, Lincoln,
do you hear something?

00:28:34.700 --> 00:28:35.533
And he was like, no.

00:28:40.610 --> 00:28:41.930
So Locke is paranoid.

00:28:41.930 --> 00:28:43.243
Locke was already scared,

00:28:43.243 --> 00:28:44.350
he'd been scared the whole time.

00:28:44.350 --> 00:28:46.728
He's kind of skittish in general.

00:28:46.728 --> 00:28:47.561
Yeah,

00:28:47.561 --> 00:28:51.320
he's scared of the dark.
He's like me.

00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:52.153
And so.

00:28:52.153 --> 00:28:54.057
He's looking over his
shoulder, not seeing anything.

00:28:54.057 --> 00:28:56.500
And then I stop,

00:28:56.500 --> 00:29:00.560
but then I walk out
into the middle of this,

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:03.091
basically, this dirt road
that they're walking down.

00:29:03.091 --> 00:29:06.454
And then I start taking
more and more steps

00:29:06.454 --> 00:29:08.460
and then right before
I really start running,

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:09.580
where they're gonna know,

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:12.222
I probably get 10 feet behind them

00:29:12.222 --> 00:29:14.374
and I was like, I'm
gonna let out the lowest,

00:29:14.374 --> 00:29:16.506
most guttural sound that I can make

00:29:16.506 --> 00:29:20.210
that will sound as unhuman as possible.

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:23.490
And I was just like.

00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:27.253
Okay.

00:29:27.253 --> 00:29:29.883
And Locke screamed,

00:29:29.883 --> 00:29:31.706
I don't think Lincoln screamed,

00:29:31.706 --> 00:29:34.563
I didn't hear him scream,
I heard Locke scream.

00:29:35.449 --> 00:29:36.982
When they tell this story,

00:29:36.982 --> 00:29:39.413
what did they say that they each thought?

00:29:39.413 --> 00:29:41.986
Locke thought it might Bigfoot,

00:29:41.986 --> 00:29:45.702
which is what I was hoping

00:29:45.702 --> 00:29:47.064
and Lincoln said

00:29:47.064 --> 00:29:51.260
that his brain was saying
he was super scared,

00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:52.847
but he was thinking if that's not Rhett,

00:29:52.847 --> 00:29:54.714
I don't know what we're gonna do.

00:29:54.714 --> 00:29:55.693
Yeah, yeah,

00:29:59.190 --> 00:30:01.610
he's more of a strategic thinker than me,

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:03.559
he knows how to play chess.

00:30:03.559 --> 00:30:04.584
Right.

00:30:04.584 --> 00:30:05.650
Yeah, so I was encouraged

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:09.987
that he didn't lose his cool completely,

00:30:09.987 --> 00:30:11.580
but I know he was scared

00:30:11.580 --> 00:30:15.727
because when they came back up

00:30:15.727 --> 00:30:18.324
and the three of you walked back,

00:30:18.324 --> 00:30:23.324
they just had this sweaty,
exasperated, wide-eyed,

00:30:24.927 --> 00:30:28.960
heart rate still pumping kind of vibe

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:30.830
and I could tell that Lincoln
was legitimately scared,

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:33.281
along with Locke, which was obvious.

00:30:33.281 --> 00:30:34.114
Listen, it's scary,

00:30:34.114 --> 00:30:37.420
it's not as scary as it was
when I was out there by myself

00:30:37.420 --> 00:30:39.592
like I told you on the solo trip,

00:30:39.592 --> 00:30:43.241
but being out there when there's
just no point of reference,

00:30:43.241 --> 00:30:46.462
there's no other people,
you just feel exposed

00:30:46.462 --> 00:30:49.483
and you don't know what
mysteries lie in the dark.

00:30:49.483 --> 00:30:51.992
I think one of my favorite
things was what happened next

00:30:51.992 --> 00:30:56.809
in that you came back
and you told that story,

00:30:56.809 --> 00:30:59.249
all three of you told the
story from your perspectives

00:30:59.249 --> 00:31:03.857
and then we went on, all of
us, sitting around the fire,

00:31:03.857 --> 00:31:05.852
you and I started telling stories,

00:31:05.852 --> 00:31:07.226
kind of building on what I was doing

00:31:07.226 --> 00:31:09.338
with the stories that I
was already telling them.

00:31:09.338 --> 00:31:10.713
Just telling the story

00:31:10.713 --> 00:31:13.957
about how we would go camping
across the Cape Fear River

00:31:13.957 --> 00:31:14.908
and the one time

00:31:14.908 --> 00:31:19.908
that you invested over
an hour of disappearing

00:31:20.617 --> 00:31:23.751
from me and the rest of the
friends that we brought camping,

00:31:23.751 --> 00:31:28.751
and fully committing to long-term
scaring the crap out of us

00:31:33.993 --> 00:31:38.993
by laying in the water
like Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:31:39.031 --> 00:31:41.483
Only my eyes and nose
were above the surface,

00:31:41.483 --> 00:31:43.660
like a gator

00:31:44.764 --> 00:31:45.947
for a long time.

00:31:45.947 --> 00:31:47.503
You know, telling 'em that story,

00:31:47.503 --> 00:31:50.726
it was just cool to see our boys

00:31:50.726 --> 00:31:53.833
so into us telling the stories

00:31:53.833 --> 00:31:55.953
and I think we ended up
telling a few stories

00:31:55.953 --> 00:31:58.751
that we had written about in
the "Book of Mythicality",

00:31:58.751 --> 00:32:00.636
and I just realized, you know what,

00:32:00.636 --> 00:32:03.734
you can't just write stories in a book

00:32:03.734 --> 00:32:05.683
and expect your kids to read 'em.

00:32:05.683 --> 00:32:07.483
These stories were still new to them.

00:32:07.483 --> 00:32:10.477
Like the story of my brother's

00:32:10.477 --> 00:32:12.108
which, in the "Book of Mythicality",

00:32:12.108 --> 00:32:13.306
it's like a comic strip,

00:32:13.306 --> 00:32:15.790
so I don't know how well it's conveyed,

00:32:15.790 --> 00:32:17.045
but it's the story of my brother

00:32:17.045 --> 00:32:19.493
in his middle school science fair project

00:32:19.493 --> 00:32:22.002
which he called "Birds",

00:32:22.002 --> 00:32:27.002
which did not have a hypothesis
or any sort of experiment.

00:32:27.693 --> 00:32:29.760
It just had a bunch of dismembered birds.

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:33.653
I think it was one bird that he had shot

00:32:33.653 --> 00:32:37.113
and then taken apart, and
put on his poster board.

00:32:38.497 --> 00:32:41.269
And, to just show you the
body parts of the birds,

00:32:41.269 --> 00:32:43.084
it definitely seems like the kind of thing

00:32:43.084 --> 00:32:43.997
a serial killer would do

00:32:43.997 --> 00:32:46.500
as a middle school science fair project,

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:48.470
but my brother is not a serial killer.

00:32:48.470 --> 00:32:49.787
And they all knew him.

00:32:49.787 --> 00:32:51.130
It was a different time.

00:32:51.130 --> 00:32:51.963
Even my kids know Cole,

00:32:51.963 --> 00:32:55.530
so they got a kick out of that
story, all of those stories.

00:32:55.530 --> 00:32:58.038
Lando in particular,
that was the conversation

00:32:58.038 --> 00:33:01.345
in which I saw Lando
check out a little bit.

00:33:01.345 --> 00:33:05.055
Yeah, he's younger, he's
such a sensitive soul

00:33:05.055 --> 00:33:08.453
and it turns out he loves birds.

00:33:12.535 --> 00:33:15.070
When you're adjusting to camping,

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:16.420
there were a number of moments

00:33:16.420 --> 00:33:17.816
when I had to pull him to the side

00:33:17.816 --> 00:33:20.993
and say, hey, we're
still having a good time.

00:33:22.537 --> 00:33:24.923
He had a number of moments
where he bounced back.

00:33:24.923 --> 00:33:26.022
He did well.
And he was good,

00:33:26.022 --> 00:33:29.242
and it was helpful that we
had the security of the van,

00:33:29.242 --> 00:33:34.242
where we could get snuggled in our blanket

00:33:34.608 --> 00:33:36.540
and feel more secure.

00:33:36.540 --> 00:33:37.543
Yeah.

00:33:39.030 --> 00:33:44.030
So the next morning, we had
to drive out of the campsite

00:33:44.083 --> 00:33:46.768
back to the main area to go and explore,

00:33:46.768 --> 00:33:48.250
and find our next campsite.

00:33:48.250 --> 00:33:50.523
And Locke,

00:33:52.611 --> 00:33:57.036
a lot of the kids these
days and kids in California,

00:33:57.036 --> 00:33:57.875
I think, especially,

00:33:57.875 --> 00:33:59.215
are on a slightly different schedule

00:33:59.215 --> 00:34:02.851
when it comes to getting their
driver's permit and license.

00:34:02.851 --> 00:34:04.954
Me and you, as soon as we turned 15,

00:34:04.954 --> 00:34:06.329
we got our driver's permit,

00:34:06.329 --> 00:34:09.394
as soon as we turned 16,
that day I got my license.

00:34:09.394 --> 00:34:10.844
Things move a little bit slower

00:34:10.844 --> 00:34:14.687
and, obviously, the pandemic
has had an effect on this.

00:34:14.687 --> 00:34:18.955
So Locke basically just
got his driver's permit

00:34:18.955 --> 00:34:20.536
right before we left

00:34:20.536 --> 00:34:23.734
and he's not really driven a car,

00:34:23.734 --> 00:34:25.017
we haven't really done the thing

00:34:25.017 --> 00:34:28.083
where you just let your
kid drive when they're 14,

00:34:28.083 --> 00:34:29.636
like, oh I'll let you drive
around the neighborhood,

00:34:29.636 --> 00:34:30.760
or something like that,

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:31.593
we just haven't done that,

00:34:31.593 --> 00:34:33.950
don't really have that
kind of neighborhood.

00:34:33.950 --> 00:34:34.800
I'm gonna sneeze.

00:34:35.966 --> 00:34:37.603
Do it in your sleeve.

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:42.023
Okay, I'm not gonna sneeze,
apparently, it went away.

00:34:44.012 --> 00:34:45.658
So he just got his permit,

00:34:45.658 --> 00:34:48.390
so I'm like, this is
the perfect opportunity

00:34:48.390 --> 00:34:49.890
for him to drive,

00:34:49.890 --> 00:34:52.128
you're in the desert,
you're on a dirt road,

00:34:52.128 --> 00:34:54.127
there's not any other traffic.

00:34:54.127 --> 00:34:54.960
I mean, in the vlog

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:57.793
where we went off-roading with Shepherd,

00:34:57.793 --> 00:34:59.803
you let him drive.

00:34:59.803 --> 00:35:00.636
Right.

00:35:00.636 --> 00:35:01.490
But he was in your lap.

00:35:01.490 --> 00:35:02.710
I was in the driver's seat still.

00:35:02.710 --> 00:35:03.543
This is for real.

00:35:03.543 --> 00:35:04.937
This is giving them 100%
control of the vehicle.

00:35:04.937 --> 00:35:06.624
But yeah, we're out there
in the middle of nowhere

00:35:06.624 --> 00:35:10.287
and it's just dirt roads leading
back up to the main road.

00:35:10.287 --> 00:35:12.245
In fact, you told me
you were gonna do that

00:35:12.245 --> 00:35:14.097
and I'm like, well, I'm
gonna go ahead far enough

00:35:14.097 --> 00:35:16.072
so that he won't be driving in my dust,

00:35:16.072 --> 00:35:17.258
so I go far ahead

00:35:17.258 --> 00:35:21.698
and I do not witness anything
that is about to happen.

00:35:21.698 --> 00:35:26.698
Okay, so this is a single lane dirt road,

00:35:28.191 --> 00:35:31.635
they come through there on a regular basis

00:35:31.635 --> 00:35:33.397
with some sort of.

00:35:33.397 --> 00:35:34.410
Scraper.

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:35.979
Scraper that pushes the dirt to the side,

00:35:35.979 --> 00:35:37.877
almost like snowbanks, right?

00:35:37.877 --> 00:35:39.600
So there's no shoulder.

00:35:39.600 --> 00:35:40.712
They're dirt banks

00:35:40.712 --> 00:35:43.610
and there's big rocks in these dirt banks.

00:35:43.610 --> 00:35:45.721
And one of the things that
happens when you start driving

00:35:45.721 --> 00:35:49.211
is you don't know where
your car is at on the road,

00:35:49.211 --> 00:35:50.807
you're on the left side

00:35:50.807 --> 00:35:52.459
and you feel like you're in the middle,

00:35:52.459 --> 00:35:55.950
but no, you have a whole
piece of car to your right.

00:35:55.950 --> 00:35:56.784
So a lot of new drivers

00:35:56.784 --> 00:35:59.050
will be hanging out to the
right a little too much,

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:01.166
I remember doing it as a new driver

00:36:01.166 --> 00:36:03.440
and Locke was doing
that almost immediately.

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:07.176
So there's some big
rocks in this embankment,

00:36:07.176 --> 00:36:08.486
I'm like, Locke, you're
too far to the right,

00:36:08.486 --> 00:36:09.643
you're too far to the right,
come back to the left,

00:36:09.643 --> 00:36:11.390
he's like, I don't think I am,

00:36:11.390 --> 00:36:12.295
I'm like, listen.

00:36:12.295 --> 00:36:15.612
And I actually said something like,

00:36:15.612 --> 00:36:18.728
all right, the only way
that this is going to work

00:36:18.728 --> 00:36:22.204
is if you assume that when
we are in the car together

00:36:22.204 --> 00:36:25.830
and we have a different view of driving,

00:36:25.830 --> 00:36:28.186
I will always be right, okay?

00:36:28.186 --> 00:36:31.018
During this stage of the relationship,

00:36:31.018 --> 00:36:32.005
that doesn't mean forever,

00:36:32.005 --> 00:36:34.495
but while you're the
pupil and I'm the teacher,

00:36:34.495 --> 00:36:36.588
the only way we can do this is just assume

00:36:36.588 --> 00:36:38.637
that I, in my driving experience,

00:36:38.637 --> 00:36:40.985
know what I'm talking about, right.

00:36:40.985 --> 00:36:45.130
And he, actually, was like,
okay, that makes sense,

00:36:45.130 --> 00:36:46.344
so I was like,

00:36:46.344 --> 00:36:48.630
and here's how you can tell
where you're at on the road.

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:51.141
Anyway, so we're going
along, he's doing great,

00:36:51.141 --> 00:36:52.649
he's doing fine,

00:36:52.649 --> 00:36:54.323
I'm having to correct him a little bit

00:36:54.323 --> 00:36:56.182
and get him to move to the center,

00:36:56.182 --> 00:36:58.561
but I'm trying to be calm.

00:36:58.561 --> 00:37:00.382
And then, all of a sudden,

00:37:00.382 --> 00:37:04.233
I see another vehicle coming towards us

00:37:04.233 --> 00:37:07.471
because, again, this is a single lane,

00:37:07.471 --> 00:37:11.353
we're gonna have to move to the
side to let each other pass.

00:37:11.353 --> 00:37:16.353
I see that the guy has
stopped and moved to the side,

00:37:17.910 --> 00:37:18.800
to let us go,

00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:20.800
he's in a Subaru of some kind,

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:22.757
not really super off-road

00:37:22.757 --> 00:37:24.654
but enough to be out on this dirt road.

00:37:24.654 --> 00:37:25.487
Okay.

00:37:25.487 --> 00:37:28.240
But Locke has seen the car
and he has come to a stop,

00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:31.611
so that guy thinks that he's
saying that he should go.

00:37:31.611 --> 00:37:32.444
Yeah.

00:37:32.444 --> 00:37:35.583
But by the time I say,
"Oh he's letting you go",

00:37:35.583 --> 00:37:38.180
Locke starts to go and
so does the other guy.

00:37:38.180 --> 00:37:39.096
It's like what you would typically do

00:37:39.096 --> 00:37:40.446
on foot with someone.

00:37:40.446 --> 00:37:41.373
Yeah, you're walking

00:37:41.373 --> 00:37:42.570
down the sidewalk.
We're both going right,

00:37:42.570 --> 00:37:45.574
we're both going left,
and we're doing a dance.

00:37:45.574 --> 00:37:47.266
You're playing Chicken at this point.

00:37:47.266 --> 00:37:49.228
What ends up happening

00:37:49.228 --> 00:37:52.246
is there's a little
bit of yelling from me.

00:37:53.496 --> 00:37:54.470
Saying something like,

00:37:54.470 --> 00:37:57.146
just go, no stop, he wants you!

00:37:57.146 --> 00:38:00.802
I'm basically embodying
the process, vocally,

00:38:00.802 --> 00:38:03.288
I wasn't helping, I'm
sure I wasn't helping.

00:38:03.288 --> 00:38:05.690
And Lincoln's in the
back seat just kind of.

00:38:05.690 --> 00:38:06.523
Smiling a little bit.

00:38:06.523 --> 00:38:07.565
Smiling.

00:38:07.565 --> 00:38:11.081
So we get to the point where it looks like

00:38:11.081 --> 00:38:14.086
we're both just gonna
drive past each other

00:38:14.086 --> 00:38:19.086
with one wheel up on this
dirt embankment on the side.

00:38:19.323 --> 00:38:22.280
You both stopped and started
how many times at this point?

00:38:22.280 --> 00:38:23.863
Four to five.

00:38:23.863 --> 00:38:26.112
That's awkward, man.

00:38:26.112 --> 00:38:27.447
That's okay,

00:38:27.447 --> 00:38:31.724
it's okay to have one
tire up on this embankment

00:38:31.724 --> 00:38:34.201
when you're in an FJ Cruiser, right.

00:38:34.201 --> 00:38:36.572
Even though Locke was
getting so far to the right

00:38:36.572 --> 00:38:38.864
that I thought he was
gonna get high centered

00:38:38.864 --> 00:38:39.697
or whatever,

00:38:39.697 --> 00:38:44.170
when, basically, you get stuck
and both wheels, you know.

00:38:44.170 --> 00:38:47.205
And so I was like, stay left,

00:38:47.205 --> 00:38:49.080
you're getting too far to the right!

00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:52.199
You know, slightly calmer than that.

00:38:52.199 --> 00:38:55.141
The other guy is doing the same thing

00:38:55.141 --> 00:38:57.582
and we eventually get past him,

00:38:57.582 --> 00:38:59.550
and then Locke gets back
in the middle and goes,

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:00.827
and then I turn around and look,

00:39:00.827 --> 00:39:03.659
and I'm like, I think that guy got stuck.

00:39:06.160 --> 00:39:08.300
He's not in a off-road vehicle

00:39:08.300 --> 00:39:10.672
and he doesn't seem to be going anywhere.

00:39:10.672 --> 00:39:11.970
And so I was like,

00:39:11.970 --> 00:39:15.240
Locke, I think that guy got stuck, stop.

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:17.376
So Locke stops, I said "Get out."

00:39:17.376 --> 00:39:20.985
I get in the driver's
seat, I put it in reverse

00:39:20.985 --> 00:39:23.777
and I back all the way up to this guy.

00:39:23.777 --> 00:39:26.720
And, sure enough, he's stuck,
but not only is he stuck,

00:39:26.720 --> 00:39:31.706
but three additional vehicles in a row,

00:39:31.706 --> 00:39:33.743
three guys who are on an off-road trip

00:39:33.743 --> 00:39:35.580
have come out of the mountain.

00:39:35.580 --> 00:39:36.598
Same place you came from.

00:39:36.598 --> 00:39:41.598
Yeah, are now stopped and
they're out talking to this guy.

00:39:42.051 --> 00:39:45.137
And then within the time
that I get out of my car,

00:39:45.137 --> 00:39:46.462
they start pushing him off

00:39:46.462 --> 00:39:48.917
and they get him off of the
embankment, and they help him.

00:39:48.917 --> 00:39:50.030
I get out of the car.

00:39:50.030 --> 00:39:52.502
I'm sure that guy said some nice things

00:39:52.502 --> 00:39:55.083
about your vehicle.

00:39:55.083 --> 00:39:56.083
When I walked up to him, I was like,

00:39:56.083 --> 00:40:00.500
hey man, hey listen,
even though Locke is 16,

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:05.500
I was like, hey, I got a
15 year old new driver,

00:40:05.568 --> 00:40:07.677
he's driving for the first
time, sorry about that

00:40:07.677 --> 00:40:09.253
and he's like, ah, no
problem, they got me,

00:40:09.253 --> 00:40:11.300
it's great, it's fine, whatever.

00:40:11.300 --> 00:40:15.850
So then I get back in the
car, I drive a little bit,

00:40:15.850 --> 00:40:17.271
I'm like, well, we still
have a lot of dirt road to go

00:40:17.271 --> 00:40:20.603
and I stop and I was like,
listen, okay, let's start over.

00:40:22.039 --> 00:40:25.433
That was something you
couldn't anticipate.

00:40:26.390 --> 00:40:27.709
It was probably your fault.

00:40:27.709 --> 00:40:30.216
And that was a difficult thing to navigate

00:40:30.216 --> 00:40:32.207
in your first driving experience,

00:40:32.207 --> 00:40:34.129
and I don't want you to be nervous

00:40:34.129 --> 00:40:35.657
and have a bad taste in your mouth

00:40:35.657 --> 00:40:37.470
on your first driving experience.

00:40:37.470 --> 00:40:38.303
So listen,

00:40:38.303 --> 00:40:39.136
let's stop.
Let me give you

00:40:39.136 --> 00:40:40.832
another bad experience.
Let's recenter,

00:40:40.832 --> 00:40:43.875
I'm gonna calm down a little
bit, you calm down a little bit

00:40:43.875 --> 00:40:45.557
because we were both
yelling at each other,

00:40:45.557 --> 00:40:49.623
we have very similar
personalities, and let's switch.

00:40:52.910 --> 00:40:54.177
So we switch, Locke gets
in the driver's seat,

00:40:57.240 --> 00:40:58.139
I get in the passenger seat,

00:40:58.139 --> 00:41:02.172
and the three guys who
have helped the Subaru

00:41:02.172 --> 00:41:05.410
have come up behind us and
stopped right behind us,

00:41:05.410 --> 00:41:08.037
so the lead car is right behind us.

00:41:08.037 --> 00:41:11.801
So Locke puts it into
gear, presses the gas,

00:41:11.801 --> 00:41:15.574
problem is the gear that
he chose was reverse.

00:41:18.401 --> 00:41:19.700
How much gas did he press?

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:23.168
Enough, enough for me to yell again.

00:41:25.414 --> 00:41:26.803
I'm telling you, man,

00:41:28.798 --> 00:41:32.167
anticipating going forward and
finding yourself in reverse.

00:41:32.167 --> 00:41:33.661
And knowing there's the
guy right behind us,

00:41:33.661 --> 00:41:34.873
I was like, Locke, Locke, Locke!

00:41:34.873 --> 00:41:37.109
I think I just said his name three times.

00:41:37.109 --> 00:41:42.109
And he ended up, I don't know
if he just hit the brakes

00:41:44.101 --> 00:41:46.340
or he just moved it back into park,

00:41:46.340 --> 00:41:50.945
either way, there was a grinding noise.

00:41:50.945 --> 00:41:51.778
Oh.

00:41:51.778 --> 00:41:53.330
From our car.

00:41:53.330 --> 00:41:55.170
We did not ram,

00:41:55.170 --> 00:41:57.780
how awful would it have been?
How close did you get,

00:41:57.780 --> 00:41:58.613
you didn't hit him?

00:41:58.613 --> 00:42:01.433
Several feet, which is
close in a car, right.

00:42:01.433 --> 00:42:04.410
And how awful would it have been for his,

00:42:04.410 --> 00:42:06.267
again, his first driving experience,

00:42:06.267 --> 00:42:08.536
to be running this guy off the road

00:42:08.536 --> 00:42:10.793
and then backing into
a guy who helped him.

00:42:12.504 --> 00:42:13.812
But after that.

00:42:13.812 --> 00:42:15.200
So at that point, what did you do?

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:17.880
He said, "Dad, I don't
wanna drive anymore."

00:42:17.880 --> 00:42:21.165
I'm sorry, I got a 13
year old driver here,

00:42:21.165 --> 00:42:25.322
he's getting younger with every mistake.

00:42:25.322 --> 00:42:27.355
And I felt for him

00:42:27.355 --> 00:42:30.681
because what I thought was gonna happen

00:42:30.681 --> 00:42:33.280
was the same thing that
happened as we drove in,

00:42:33.280 --> 00:42:34.541
which was no other cars,

00:42:34.541 --> 00:42:35.909
but on the way out

00:42:35.909 --> 00:42:37.467
we have to run against
this person coming at us

00:42:37.467 --> 00:42:39.041
and we've got three guys behind us.

00:42:39.041 --> 00:42:40.439
So did you switch back at that point?

00:42:40.439 --> 00:42:42.773
Switched back and I drove out.

00:42:43.694 --> 00:42:46.176
He actually did not end up driving again,

00:42:46.176 --> 00:42:47.275
that wasn't by design,

00:42:47.275 --> 00:42:49.670
that was more related to
what ended up happening,

00:42:49.670 --> 00:42:51.493
which we'll talk about next.

00:42:54.333 --> 00:42:55.207
I was waiting for you

00:42:55.207 --> 00:42:57.631
and I was like, why are they not here yet?

00:42:57.631 --> 00:42:59.382
He gave me permission to tell that story.

00:42:59.382 --> 00:43:01.150
And I was like, listen,

00:43:01.150 --> 00:43:02.587
and, as we were driving out,

00:43:02.587 --> 00:43:03.676
we actually had a good conversation,

00:43:03.676 --> 00:43:06.425
I was like, listen,
man, this kind of thing,

00:43:06.425 --> 00:43:11.425
this is why you do this,
so you can have a story.

00:43:11.809 --> 00:43:13.631
Or so your dad can have a story,

00:43:13.631 --> 00:43:16.283
you get a podcast one day,
you can have the story.

00:43:16.283 --> 00:43:19.869
No, I was like, you've got
your first driving story.

00:43:19.869 --> 00:43:20.726
And second.

00:43:20.726 --> 00:43:21.559
Yeah, first and second.

00:43:21.559 --> 00:43:24.195
I'd call that two stories.
Two for one,

00:43:24.195 --> 00:43:27.215
and you're safe, no one got hurt,

00:43:27.215 --> 00:43:28.723
we can have a laugh about it,

00:43:28.723 --> 00:43:31.022
you learn something, you move on,

00:43:31.022 --> 00:43:34.668
and we actually got to a good place.

00:43:34.668 --> 00:43:38.000
You catch up with us, I hear the story

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:42.072
and then we ended up driving
all the way out of the valley

00:43:42.072 --> 00:43:44.701
to Beatty to refuel,

00:43:44.701 --> 00:43:46.472
and then we knew there
was a ghost town there

00:43:46.472 --> 00:43:48.932
that we dropped in on, we ate some lunch,

00:43:48.932 --> 00:43:53.270
then we came back into
the canyon, Titus Canyon,

00:43:53.270 --> 00:43:56.200
which is one of the more popular drives,

00:43:56.200 --> 00:43:57.271
but, again, that time of the year,

00:43:57.271 --> 00:44:00.707
not that many people there, great drive.

00:44:00.707 --> 00:44:02.307
The best drive.

00:44:02.307 --> 00:44:06.203
Like, again, it's not technical,

00:44:07.391 --> 00:44:09.421
it's best to have high clearance,

00:44:09.421 --> 00:44:11.721
but no problem at all for the van.

00:44:11.721 --> 00:44:13.680
We saw a guy in a Maxima.

00:44:13.680 --> 00:44:14.513
We did.

00:44:14.513 --> 00:44:16.023
And it's a one-way thing.

00:44:16.890 --> 00:44:18.067
Do not recommend that.

00:44:18.067 --> 00:44:20.590
It's got a lot of wash boarding.

00:44:20.590 --> 00:44:21.917
But there was a Maxima there.

00:44:21.917 --> 00:44:24.677
Your car is shaking as you go.

00:44:24.677 --> 00:44:27.290
Also saw a guy on a bike
doing the whole thing,

00:44:27.290 --> 00:44:28.123
that's nuts.

00:44:28.123 --> 00:44:31.910
There is this, and this is
where I got drone footage,

00:44:31.910 --> 00:44:33.791
I haven't looked at it,

00:44:33.791 --> 00:44:34.757
it probably won't be that impressive.

00:44:34.757 --> 00:44:39.195
But as you come down out of
the mountains and the canyon,

00:44:39.195 --> 00:44:44.195
you're on one of those ice
road truckers sort of roads

00:44:45.066 --> 00:44:47.700
in which, obviously, there's no guardrail

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:51.030
and there's just a sheer drop
into a canyon on one side,

00:44:51.030 --> 00:44:54.487
and it's one lane wide
and that's exciting,

00:44:54.487 --> 00:44:55.948
we had a good time.

00:44:55.948 --> 00:44:58.840
And then at the end you
get into the slot canyon.

00:44:58.840 --> 00:44:59.920
Amazing.

00:44:59.920 --> 00:45:02.536
Where you're literally
driving in a canyon.

00:45:02.536 --> 00:45:04.070
The width of the car.

00:45:04.070 --> 00:45:05.190
Is the width of a road

00:45:05.190 --> 00:45:08.506
with straight, stone walls.
The width of a single lane

00:45:08.506 --> 00:45:09.572
of a road.
Yeah, right.

00:45:09.572 --> 00:45:10.405
One way.

00:45:10.405 --> 00:45:11.915
And it's not just straight.

00:45:11.915 --> 00:45:13.430
Winding back and forth.
It winds back and forth,

00:45:13.430 --> 00:45:18.137
so you can never see more
than 15 yards in front of you.

00:45:18.137 --> 00:45:19.450
And that's why it's one way.

00:45:19.450 --> 00:45:21.107
Yeah, that bottleneck.

00:45:21.107 --> 00:45:21.974
Yeah.

00:45:21.974 --> 00:45:23.155
So we go through that

00:45:23.155 --> 00:45:26.277
and it was really cool to go with the boys

00:45:26.277 --> 00:45:27.471
and to have these experiences.

00:45:27.471 --> 00:45:29.360
For the first part of that trip,

00:45:29.360 --> 00:45:30.768
Lando, actually, was asleep in the back,

00:45:30.768 --> 00:45:33.338
I don't know how he did
it, it was so bumpy.

00:45:33.338 --> 00:45:35.635
We were worried

00:45:35.635 --> 00:45:37.695
and there was a couple of places

00:45:37.695 --> 00:45:40.710
where Locke would be like, dad, dad,

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:41.820
you sure about this?

00:45:41.820 --> 00:45:43.542
'Cause it freaks you out,

00:45:43.542 --> 00:45:47.700
'cause you're like, well, if a rock falls.

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:50.460
If you reach down and
change the radio station,

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:52.736
if you got a radio station at that point,

00:45:52.736 --> 00:45:54.167
then you could careen off
the side of the thing.

00:45:54.167 --> 00:45:56.416
All systems are online
when you are doing this.

00:45:56.416 --> 00:45:57.249
Absolutely.

00:45:57.249 --> 00:45:59.510
And Lincoln was like, I
don't know how Lando's doing.

00:45:59.510 --> 00:46:01.377
That's a good point,
well, he was sleeping.

00:46:01.377 --> 00:46:02.400
He was sleeping.

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:03.285
That's the miracle of it all.

00:46:03.285 --> 00:46:05.949
So I got Shep to sit in
the front seat with me

00:46:05.949 --> 00:46:08.703
and I'm like, you like Steely Dan?

00:46:09.676 --> 00:46:11.600
Shepherd's an old soul.

00:46:11.600 --> 00:46:13.293
You've been training him
on what to listen to,

00:46:13.293 --> 00:46:14.796
he didn't know them by name,

00:46:14.796 --> 00:46:17.523
but we were listening to some Dan.

00:46:18.407 --> 00:46:22.995
I really feel like we had
this kind of uncle moment,

00:46:22.995 --> 00:46:25.800
it was cool for all of us to hang out

00:46:25.800 --> 00:46:27.760
and, incidentally,

00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:32.135
Lincoln told me about sleeping
over at the house with Locke,

00:46:32.135 --> 00:46:34.393
subsequent to this trip.

00:46:35.297 --> 00:46:36.639
Everything he told me that he did,

00:46:36.639 --> 00:46:41.000
he was like, me and Locke
and Rhett, we ate this

00:46:41.000 --> 00:46:45.020
and then me and Locke and
Rhett, we watched a movie,

00:46:45.020 --> 00:46:47.261
I was like, it sounds
like you were hanging out

00:46:47.261 --> 00:46:49.920
with Locke and Rhett the whole time,

00:46:49.920 --> 00:46:51.680
I felt a little threatened,

00:46:51.680 --> 00:46:53.815
I was like, you know what,
this is good uncle vibes.

00:46:53.815 --> 00:46:58.795
We watched just a heinous horror movie.

00:46:58.795 --> 00:47:00.103
He told me.

00:47:03.739 --> 00:47:06.037
That trip really fostered

00:47:06.037 --> 00:47:08.263
an extended family type of connection

00:47:08.263 --> 00:47:11.794
and that's just the magic of camping,

00:47:11.794 --> 00:47:13.676
is that you find yourself
around a campfire

00:47:13.676 --> 00:47:15.033
and you can over glamorize it,

00:47:15.033 --> 00:47:16.650
and, trust me, there's a lot of stuff

00:47:16.650 --> 00:47:18.297
that, the majority of
time, is not glamorized,

00:47:18.297 --> 00:47:21.100
but then you find those
moments that you can hold onto,

00:47:21.100 --> 00:47:23.030
like the Steely Dan moment

00:47:23.030 --> 00:47:25.381
when it's this amazing view,

00:47:25.381 --> 00:47:27.268
you guys were way ahead of us

00:47:27.268 --> 00:47:29.980
and then you had the drone
by the time we caught up.

00:47:29.980 --> 00:47:33.723
And then you just stop
at this, a ghost town,

00:47:33.723 --> 00:47:36.089
there was a place called Lead Field

00:47:36.089 --> 00:47:37.847
which is just a place that sprung up

00:47:37.847 --> 00:47:39.851
when they thought they
were gonna be mining lead

00:47:39.851 --> 00:47:40.940
and it turns out

00:47:40.940 --> 00:47:43.658
that the place had a
post office for 11 months

00:47:43.658 --> 00:47:45.780
and 300 people lived there.

00:47:45.780 --> 00:47:47.250
And they've got the pictures out there

00:47:47.250 --> 00:47:48.083
of what it used to look like,

00:47:48.083 --> 00:47:49.887
and there's still three buildings standing

00:47:49.887 --> 00:47:52.118
that you can walk around.

00:47:52.118 --> 00:47:53.180
The mines are closed off.

00:47:53.180 --> 00:47:54.110
The mines are closed

00:47:54.110 --> 00:47:54.980
but you can walk up to 'em and look in,

00:47:54.980 --> 00:47:56.221
and they've got 'em

00:47:56.221 --> 00:47:57.346
marked off.
Yeah, super cool.

00:47:57.346 --> 00:47:59.576
And the kids were really into this

00:47:59.576 --> 00:48:02.017
and us talking about what
would it have been like

00:48:02.017 --> 00:48:06.704
to be out here 120 years ago, mining lead?

00:48:06.704 --> 00:48:07.990
You know?

00:48:07.990 --> 00:48:09.830
I mean, we come back and we're
telling all these stories

00:48:09.830 --> 00:48:13.623
and Lily's like, I kind
of wish I would have gone

00:48:13.623 --> 00:48:15.787
and I was like, girl,
you can go next time.

00:48:15.787 --> 00:48:19.395
But it was cool for her
to say that out loud.

00:48:19.395 --> 00:48:20.931
'Cause she was definitely not down.

00:48:20.931 --> 00:48:22.399
Yeah, she wasn't interested

00:48:22.399 --> 00:48:24.241
and her and Christy were hanging out,

00:48:24.241 --> 00:48:26.750
and it was good for them.

00:48:26.750 --> 00:48:28.899
So we leave Lead Field and, again,

00:48:28.899 --> 00:48:31.889
you go through that slot
canyon at the end of that trip

00:48:31.889 --> 00:48:35.073
and then you're like, man,
we're chasing daylight here,

00:48:35.073 --> 00:48:38.937
we gotta find a place
to camp for the night.

00:48:38.937 --> 00:48:41.445
And, again, we didn't wanna
be in the middle of the valley

00:48:41.445 --> 00:48:45.798
where there are some
pockets of civilization,

00:48:45.798 --> 00:48:48.146
like a gas station and a campground,

00:48:48.146 --> 00:48:52.595
and one hotel with a bunch of palm trees,

00:48:52.595 --> 00:48:54.410
which is so super strange.

00:48:54.410 --> 00:48:55.243
I would say a resort.

00:48:55.243 --> 00:48:57.470
Yeah, a resort, it's strange.

00:48:57.470 --> 00:48:58.303
Super nice.

00:48:58.303 --> 00:49:01.390
The only one they let in there to develop

00:49:01.390 --> 00:49:02.925
or maybe the only people willing to do it

00:49:02.925 --> 00:49:04.290
and to keep it up.

00:49:04.290 --> 00:49:06.343
But, anyway, we actually drive past that

00:49:06.343 --> 00:49:07.998
and you had found, on the map,

00:49:07.998 --> 00:49:10.566
if we go up this canyon
called Echo Canyon,

00:49:10.566 --> 00:49:15.566
we start going up that on
that high clearance road.

00:49:16.742 --> 00:49:19.308
A lot of people camping there

00:49:19.308 --> 00:49:22.656
and there was a truck in front
of us that was going so slow,

00:49:22.656 --> 00:49:24.083
he let us go by,

00:49:24.083 --> 00:49:27.198
and then I'm like, man,
we've been driving all day,

00:49:27.198 --> 00:49:29.312
my shoulders are sore, I am exhausted,

00:49:29.312 --> 00:49:33.416
I just feel like I can't
steer this thing anymore.

00:49:33.416 --> 00:49:35.534
I'm feeling every bump in the road,

00:49:35.534 --> 00:49:38.398
I'm glad we're almost
to finding a campsite.

00:49:38.398 --> 00:49:42.565
And with every rock we hit and
every turn we tried to make,

00:49:42.565 --> 00:49:47.040
I was like, man, if I didn't know better,

00:49:47.040 --> 00:49:51.342
this feels like my first pickup truck,

00:49:51.342 --> 00:49:54.975
a 1987 Nissan pickup,
had no power steering.

00:49:54.975 --> 00:49:55.861
Yeah.
I was like,

00:49:55.861 --> 00:49:57.600
that's what this feels like.

00:49:57.600 --> 00:49:59.404
And then I was like, hold on,

00:49:59.404 --> 00:50:02.554
I think that might be what happened.

00:50:02.554 --> 00:50:03.720
The thing we haven't talked about.

00:50:03.720 --> 00:50:04.854
And it's getting dark.

00:50:04.854 --> 00:50:07.593
We were talking on our walkie talkies,

00:50:08.642 --> 00:50:10.810
having a walkie talkie.

00:50:10.810 --> 00:50:11.643
Oh yeah.

00:50:11.643 --> 00:50:14.540
Being able to, hey, big boy, over.

00:50:14.540 --> 00:50:16.490
Little boy, over.

00:50:16.490 --> 00:50:19.001
Talking back and forth to each other,

00:50:19.001 --> 00:50:21.771
that's the way to go on a
off-road trip like that.

00:50:21.771 --> 00:50:23.190
There was lots of checking in

00:50:23.190 --> 00:50:24.654
and also lots of walkie jokes.

00:50:24.654 --> 00:50:26.350
Walkie jokes, yeah.

00:50:26.350 --> 00:50:29.128
If you'll look to your left right now,

00:50:29.128 --> 00:50:31.833
you'll see a big pair of
boobies, things like that.

00:50:31.833 --> 00:50:33.169
It's just rocks.

00:50:33.169 --> 00:50:34.765
Yeah, you gotta make those kinds of jokes

00:50:34.765 --> 00:50:35.890
when the boys are around.

00:50:35.890 --> 00:50:36.940
Just squint a little bit.

00:50:36.940 --> 00:50:38.433
Yeah, right.

00:50:38.433 --> 00:50:39.937
I say that 'cause you were on the walkie

00:50:39.937 --> 00:50:43.686
and you were like, I'm
having trouble steering.

00:50:43.686 --> 00:50:44.545
I'm tired.

00:50:44.545 --> 00:50:47.052
I think there might be something wrong

00:50:47.052 --> 00:50:48.270
with my power steering.

00:50:48.270 --> 00:50:50.113
And, I'll be honest with
you, when you said that,

00:50:50.113 --> 00:50:55.113
I was like, I just think he must be tired,

00:50:56.059 --> 00:50:57.766
there's nothing wrong with that,

00:50:57.766 --> 00:51:02.766
there's nothing wrong with
that brand new, super nice van.

00:51:02.949 --> 00:51:04.952
It's getting harder by
the second to steer,

00:51:04.952 --> 00:51:07.231
it's getting darker by the second.

00:51:07.231 --> 00:51:09.015
We're having to pass
all these people camping

00:51:09.015 --> 00:51:10.156
and then we.

00:51:10.156 --> 00:51:11.546
Go up in a slot canyon.

00:51:11.546 --> 00:51:12.462
We go in a slot canyon

00:51:12.462 --> 00:51:14.751
and we found this super cool campsite.

00:51:14.751 --> 00:51:15.610
Oh man.

00:51:15.610 --> 00:51:16.758
And you hop out and I hop out,

00:51:16.758 --> 00:51:20.201
and I'm like, man, I swear,
I've lost power steering,

00:51:20.201 --> 00:51:22.020
I'm losing power steering.

00:51:22.020 --> 00:51:22.853
And you look under the front

00:51:22.853 --> 00:51:25.494
and something's leaking,
it's wet under there,

00:51:25.494 --> 00:51:27.916
that's exactly what it is.

00:51:27.916 --> 00:51:29.623
It's a scary feeling,

00:51:29.623 --> 00:51:30.833
'cause we had gotten to the point

00:51:30.833 --> 00:51:33.549
where we felt that isolation,

00:51:33.549 --> 00:51:35.765
'cause that's where we like to camp.

00:51:35.765 --> 00:51:39.275
And it was just, there's
this sinking feeling

00:51:39.275 --> 00:51:42.723
of this could get bad
real quick, you know?

00:51:42.723 --> 00:51:46.665
'Cause it's like, it'd be so
hard to get a vehicle in here,

00:51:46.665 --> 00:51:49.814
like a tow truck in here
to get this thing out.

00:51:49.814 --> 00:51:52.470
So I'm like, we can't camp here,

00:51:52.470 --> 00:51:56.050
we gotta get this thing
back out of here right now.

00:51:56.050 --> 00:51:58.090
If it's leaking power steering

00:51:58.090 --> 00:52:00.919
before it's all bone dry,

00:52:00.919 --> 00:52:05.135
which could potentially
do damage to the vehicle,

00:52:05.135 --> 00:52:07.106
I gotta try to get this thing back out,

00:52:07.106 --> 00:52:08.037
closer to civilization.

00:52:08.037 --> 00:52:11.264
So we turn right back around
and go all the way back out,

00:52:11.264 --> 00:52:12.882
and by the time we got back out,

00:52:12.882 --> 00:52:15.771
I'm like, hanging a right
onto the paved road,

00:52:15.771 --> 00:52:20.771
I'm trying with all of my
might to turn the wheel

00:52:21.012 --> 00:52:22.373
to get this thing back.

00:52:22.373 --> 00:52:24.369
'Cause this is not an
'87 Nissan pickup truck.

00:52:24.369 --> 00:52:25.670
No, it's not.

00:52:25.670 --> 00:52:27.074
This is a big vehicle.

00:52:27.074 --> 00:52:32.074
We drove back down into the
valley, to a campground,

00:52:32.206 --> 00:52:33.968
which said it was totally full.

00:52:33.968 --> 00:52:35.770
Furnace Creek Campground,

00:52:35.770 --> 00:52:40.733
'cause I wanna give a
shout out to the rangers,

00:52:40.733 --> 00:52:43.725
the park rangers who were at the camp.

00:52:43.725 --> 00:52:44.790
'Cause there were some Mythical Beasts.

00:52:44.790 --> 00:52:46.370
There were Mythical Beasts, yeah.

00:52:46.370 --> 00:52:47.203
But the night before,

00:52:47.203 --> 00:52:49.007
we talked to the guy who
lives there permanently,

00:52:49.007 --> 00:52:51.314
older guy who was like,
well, they didn't show up,

00:52:51.314 --> 00:52:52.297
you can stay in this reserved slot.

00:52:52.297 --> 00:52:53.480
Lyle.

00:52:53.480 --> 00:52:56.520
Lyle, love you Lyle.

00:52:56.520 --> 00:52:58.537
So we're like, you know what?

00:52:58.537 --> 00:53:02.006
I can still camp in
this thing for tonight,

00:53:02.006 --> 00:53:05.251
I called the owners or texted the owners,

00:53:05.251 --> 00:53:06.687
I told 'em what was up.

00:53:06.687 --> 00:53:09.435
They were understanding
and helped me problem solve

00:53:09.435 --> 00:53:12.274
in terms of what we're
gonna do with this thing.

00:53:12.274 --> 00:53:15.181
They said, well, since you're situated,

00:53:15.181 --> 00:53:16.741
we'll have to find out the next morning

00:53:16.741 --> 00:53:18.417
what the best course of action is

00:53:18.417 --> 00:53:21.173
in calling the roadside assistance.

00:53:22.338 --> 00:53:24.413
So that night was,

00:53:25.657 --> 00:53:28.606
we were able to have an actual fire there,

00:53:28.606 --> 00:53:29.991
not with the propane fire.

00:53:29.991 --> 00:53:34.375
I went to the fancy general store,

00:53:34.375 --> 00:53:39.375
which was at a little outdoor
mall resort kind of situation

00:53:39.408 --> 00:53:40.271
in the middle of the desert,

00:53:40.271 --> 00:53:44.653
to get this firewood that
costs me $13 a bundle

00:53:48.067 --> 00:53:50.100
and I got three bundles,

00:53:50.100 --> 00:53:51.450
that's some expensive wood.

00:53:52.350 --> 00:53:54.680
We had a good night that night.

00:53:54.680 --> 00:53:55.839
The next day we find out

00:53:55.839 --> 00:53:57.842
that no, they can't come out and fix it,

00:53:57.842 --> 00:53:59.300
this is such a new vehicle,

00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:02.038
they're gonna have to come
tow it to the nearest place,

00:54:02.038 --> 00:54:03.189
which is Las Vegas.

00:54:03.189 --> 00:54:04.380
And can I say that we,

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:09.380
you talked about our non prowess

00:54:10.023 --> 00:54:14.477
when it comes to diagnosing
vehicular problems,

00:54:14.477 --> 00:54:16.279
but I say, between the two of us.

00:54:16.279 --> 00:54:17.320
We were right about that.

00:54:17.320 --> 00:54:20.034
We thought what had happened was

00:54:20.034 --> 00:54:23.332
is that the hose to the pump
had completely come loose,

00:54:23.332 --> 00:54:24.601
based on the spray pattern.

00:54:24.601 --> 00:54:26.453
Oh, forensics.

00:54:26.453 --> 00:54:29.248
Of the power steering fluid,

00:54:29.248 --> 00:54:30.320
and it turns out,

00:54:30.320 --> 00:54:32.777
that's exactly what had
happened to the car.

00:54:32.777 --> 00:54:34.393
I'll fast forward,

00:54:35.981 --> 00:54:38.713
so we arranged for the tow truck to come

00:54:38.713 --> 00:54:41.040
and then the owner said
"We're just gonna get 'em

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:43.080
to tow it all the way
back to Los Angeles",

00:54:43.080 --> 00:54:44.134
three and a half hours?

00:54:44.134 --> 00:54:45.706
From there it was four and a half hours.

00:54:45.706 --> 00:54:47.338
Four and a half hours.

00:54:47.338 --> 00:54:51.410
And the tow truck came
from LA, picked it up,

00:54:51.410 --> 00:54:53.010
and then went the four
and a half hours back.

00:54:53.010 --> 00:54:54.191
And it couldn't be fixed

00:54:54.191 --> 00:54:56.534
because the clamp is a proprietary clamp

00:54:56.534 --> 00:55:00.890
that you found out very
soon after, that Mercedes.

00:55:00.890 --> 00:55:02.421
People talk about it online.

00:55:02.421 --> 00:55:03.983
Had issued a recall.

00:55:03.983 --> 00:55:05.995
Well, that's what I
found out, weeks later.

00:55:05.995 --> 00:55:07.220
So, just to close that loop,

00:55:07.220 --> 00:55:09.894
yeah, whenever I was
returning stuff to the owner

00:55:09.894 --> 00:55:11.394
that I'd taken out of the van,

00:55:11.394 --> 00:55:13.517
she was like, I just got the notice today,

00:55:13.517 --> 00:55:16.045
the recall for them to fix that.

00:55:16.045 --> 00:55:18.206
So I know I didn't do
anything inappropriate,

00:55:18.206 --> 00:55:19.722
but it made me feel better

00:55:19.722 --> 00:55:23.373
that like, hey, this is a
Mercedes problem, not a.

00:55:23.373 --> 00:55:25.173
A Link problem.
A Link problem.

00:55:26.165 --> 00:55:28.062
But that took most of our day

00:55:28.062 --> 00:55:30.335
'cause we had to drive all the way out,

00:55:30.335 --> 00:55:35.335
you had to drive me out to the
nearest place to rent a car,

00:55:36.333 --> 00:55:37.837
all the way out of the valley

00:55:37.837 --> 00:55:41.540
and rent a minivan,
bring it all the way in,

00:55:41.540 --> 00:55:43.390
and so we kinda lost a day.

00:55:43.390 --> 00:55:44.223
Significant downgrade

00:55:44.223 --> 00:55:46.288
in how cool you seemed
the rest of the trip.

00:55:46.288 --> 00:55:47.121
Yeah.

00:55:47.121 --> 00:55:50.858
You went from having
a van that was so big.

00:55:50.858 --> 00:55:54.065
#vanlife goes to #minivanlife,
totally different life.

00:55:54.065 --> 00:55:56.780
The minivan life was not,

00:55:56.780 --> 00:55:57.873
I didn't wanna be in pictures

00:55:57.873 --> 00:55:59.411
with you and your minivan anymore.

00:55:59.411 --> 00:56:02.469
I took a picture of the Sprinter

00:56:02.469 --> 00:56:04.100
being loaded up on the ramp.

00:56:04.100 --> 00:56:06.234
I got that on video, too.

00:56:06.234 --> 00:56:07.521
Oh, cool, drone footage?

00:56:07.521 --> 00:56:09.920
No drone, but I got that on video.

00:56:09.920 --> 00:56:12.023
I wanna see that, that's cool.

00:56:13.270 --> 00:56:15.370
Last story we gotta tell.

00:56:15.370 --> 00:56:16.470
We had enough time.

00:56:16.470 --> 00:56:20.630
Is we all hopped in the
minivan as the sun was setting

00:56:20.630 --> 00:56:21.497
and we drove to this one canyon,

00:56:21.497 --> 00:56:26.497
it was called Sunset Canyon
or something like that

00:56:27.205 --> 00:56:30.613
and we knew it was gonna get dark,

00:56:31.750 --> 00:56:34.205
that meant I grabbed a jacket,

00:56:34.205 --> 00:56:37.326
but it didn't mean that
I grabbed flashlights

00:56:37.326 --> 00:56:39.953
or headlamps, or anything,
'cause we did not do that.

00:56:39.953 --> 00:56:44.036
Right, but, as it was
getting dark, it was clear

00:56:44.036 --> 00:56:46.100
and me and Locke were, actually,

00:56:46.100 --> 00:56:47.015
we kinda got up ahead of you guys

00:56:47.015 --> 00:56:49.134
and we were walking ahead of everybody.

00:56:49.134 --> 00:56:53.377
And, again, it was this crazy canyon

00:56:53.377 --> 00:56:57.903
that's got this chalky white rock,

00:56:57.903 --> 00:57:00.244
that's almost like dirt meets rock,

00:57:00.244 --> 00:57:02.213
like dirt that hasn't yet become rock,

00:57:02.213 --> 00:57:07.213
so, sedimentary rock that is
still very sedimenty, right.

00:57:07.400 --> 00:57:10.647
And a lot of people are walking
out as we're walking in,

00:57:10.647 --> 00:57:13.131
up into this, basically
another slot canyon.

00:57:13.131 --> 00:57:15.662
But you could see that the
moon was gonna be bright

00:57:15.662 --> 00:57:18.096
and, actually, as the sun was going down

00:57:18.096 --> 00:57:19.294
and the moon was coming up,

00:57:19.294 --> 00:57:21.963
you could already see your moon shadow.

00:57:21.963 --> 00:57:22.796
Pretty crazy.

00:57:22.796 --> 00:57:24.643
And I was like, oh, this is gonna be fine,

00:57:24.643 --> 00:57:26.846
we don't need lights,
let's just keep going.

00:57:26.846 --> 00:57:29.231
I will say, when it was all said and done,

00:57:29.231 --> 00:57:30.664
I'm like, Lando, what
was your favorite thing?

00:57:30.664 --> 00:57:32.428
He was like, the night hike.

00:57:32.428 --> 00:57:34.180
'Cause, at a certain point,
we started calling this,

00:57:34.180 --> 00:57:37.136
all right, guys, this is the
night hike, we're doing it,

00:57:37.136 --> 00:57:38.590
you know.

00:57:39.690 --> 00:57:41.299
And we went in a few miles,

00:57:41.299 --> 00:57:44.956
you get up against this
sheer mountain faces

00:57:44.956 --> 00:57:46.654
they call it the cathedral.

00:57:46.654 --> 00:57:48.414
The Red Cathedral, yeah.

00:57:48.414 --> 00:57:52.263
There's red rock wall.

00:57:53.450 --> 00:57:56.696
And you and Locke had
gotten a lot farther ahead

00:57:56.696 --> 00:57:58.210
and, matter of fact, I convinced myself

00:57:58.210 --> 00:58:00.762
that you guys were gonna try
to scare the crap out of us

00:58:00.762 --> 00:58:03.102
and I was not that happy about that.

00:58:03.102 --> 00:58:05.365
But when we got to the cathedral.

00:58:05.365 --> 00:58:06.205
You and Locke?

00:58:06.205 --> 00:58:08.852
Yeah, so we got to the
end of the end of the line

00:58:08.852 --> 00:58:12.325
and there was a cave
you had to walk through

00:58:12.325 --> 00:58:14.648
to get to the final end of the trail.

00:58:14.648 --> 00:58:19.276
And we could hear, this is so weird,

00:58:19.276 --> 00:58:22.150
we could hear you guys
talking to each other.

00:58:22.150 --> 00:58:22.983
Crisp?

00:58:22.983 --> 00:58:26.292
Like I could make out the
words that you were saying.

00:58:26.292 --> 00:58:28.344
And so I wanted to make sure

00:58:28.344 --> 00:58:29.177
that you were gonna come all the way

00:58:29.177 --> 00:58:31.101
and you weren't gonna turn around.

00:58:31.101 --> 00:58:34.993
And so I was like, Link, I
yelled as loud as I could.

00:58:34.993 --> 00:58:36.190
Didn't hear anything.

00:58:36.190 --> 00:58:37.023
No response.

00:58:37.023 --> 00:58:39.030
But you could hear me
just talking normally.

00:58:39.030 --> 00:58:42.266
Some acoustic issue,

00:58:42.266 --> 00:58:44.853
where I couldn't get my voice to go to you

00:58:44.853 --> 00:58:45.873
and I think it might be

00:58:45.873 --> 00:58:50.677
because you were in this little cave thing

00:58:50.677 --> 00:58:51.821
and it was coming out of the cave,

00:58:51.821 --> 00:58:54.287
but I couldn't get my
voice to go into the cave.

00:58:54.287 --> 00:58:57.323
Rhett, no one will ever
know, it's a mystery.

00:58:57.323 --> 00:58:58.278
It's an acoustic mystery.

00:58:58.278 --> 00:58:59.560
It's an acoustic mystery.

00:58:59.560 --> 00:59:02.010
That keeps me up.
That keeps you up at night,

00:59:02.010 --> 00:59:02.843
you're thinking about it.

00:59:02.843 --> 00:59:03.775
But then you finally show up.

00:59:03.775 --> 00:59:05.051
And it's pretty cool

00:59:05.051 --> 00:59:06.828
because, at this point,

00:59:06.828 --> 00:59:09.324
you go into the sheer
wall of the cathedral

00:59:09.324 --> 00:59:10.693
and then you double back

00:59:10.693 --> 00:59:15.693
and go up this pyramid
of dirt to this ridge

00:59:18.445 --> 00:59:21.438
that, then, we started
taking pictures of the kids.

00:59:21.438 --> 00:59:23.336
I took a silhouette picture of the kids.

00:59:23.336 --> 00:59:27.666
Backlit by the moonlight
and it was super cool.

00:59:27.666 --> 00:59:32.284
And so I'm down below, you're
up a little bit ahead of me,

00:59:32.284 --> 00:59:35.084
taking a picture of all
of the boys up there

00:59:35.084 --> 00:59:36.323
on the ridge

00:59:36.323 --> 00:59:38.830
and then we take the pictures
and we're like, all right,

00:59:38.830 --> 00:59:40.058
let's start heading back down.

00:59:40.058 --> 00:59:44.303
And just as we start heading back down,

00:59:44.303 --> 00:59:48.821
there's some sort of a, there's
a little bit of a kerfuffle,

00:59:48.821 --> 00:59:50.740
there's something going on,

00:59:50.740 --> 00:59:53.099
there's some shimmying
happening and some grunting

00:59:53.099 --> 00:59:56.429
as people are starting to come down.

00:59:56.429 --> 00:59:59.373
And the first thing that I hear said,

01:00:01.824 --> 01:00:04.985
I could tell people were concerned

01:00:04.985 --> 01:00:09.985
and Lando says, please,
nobody say any bad words.

01:00:11.760 --> 01:00:15.590
And so I'm like, okay, what has happened?

01:00:15.590 --> 01:00:18.442
Well, apparently he had
picked up on a pattern,

01:00:18.442 --> 01:00:19.754
which is if things went wrong,

01:00:19.754 --> 01:00:22.188
there were bad words that were said.

01:00:22.188 --> 01:00:23.974
Please nobody say any bad words.

01:00:23.974 --> 01:00:25.340
That upset him.

01:00:25.340 --> 01:00:26.626
I was like, what's happened?

01:00:26.626 --> 01:00:30.349
It turns out what had
happened was Shepherd,

01:00:30.349 --> 01:00:33.336
who let me just go on record and say

01:00:33.336 --> 01:00:38.336
that Shepherd, actually,
has a great track record

01:00:38.627 --> 01:00:40.737
with keeping up with his phone,

01:00:40.737 --> 01:00:43.426
not so much with my other son,

01:00:43.426 --> 01:00:45.784
but great track record of
keeping up with his phone

01:00:45.784 --> 01:00:47.655
and not losing things.

01:00:47.655 --> 01:00:52.655
But Shepherd had dropped
his relatively new phone

01:00:53.087 --> 01:00:58.087
that had proceeded to
slide down this cliffside,

01:00:58.860 --> 01:01:01.233
I mean, we're talking a 45 degree angle

01:01:01.233 --> 01:01:03.406
that just kind of went down

01:01:03.406 --> 01:01:05.719
into the lower part of the Red Cathedral.

01:01:05.719 --> 01:01:08.327
Which was a dark abyss, really.

01:01:08.327 --> 01:01:11.090
And we could not see anything.

01:01:11.090 --> 01:01:14.222
And even when we shined our
phone lights onto the slope

01:01:14.222 --> 01:01:16.721
we were like, there's no way
that we're gonna find this,

01:01:16.721 --> 01:01:21.156
but it felt like the phone
has gotta be right there.

01:01:21.156 --> 01:01:21.989
And so.

01:01:21.989 --> 01:01:23.279
Did you say any bad words?

01:01:23.279 --> 01:01:25.922
I didn't say any bad words,
'cause Lando said not to.

01:01:25.922 --> 01:01:27.712
I don't think that's true.

01:01:27.712 --> 01:01:29.810
No, I said one, what did I say?

01:01:29.810 --> 01:01:32.796
I think after looking for about 15 minutes

01:01:32.796 --> 01:01:37.363
and crab walking down
that 45 degree slope,

01:01:37.363 --> 01:01:38.832
and kind of feeling like

01:01:38.832 --> 01:01:41.514
you don't know what you could tumble into,

01:01:41.514 --> 01:01:45.306
you kinda started getting
a little bit more grouchy.

01:01:45.306 --> 01:01:48.171
Well, here's the thing.

01:01:48.171 --> 01:01:49.582
And I'd walked down below,

01:01:49.582 --> 01:01:52.959
'cause I was down at the bottom
and you were up at the top,

01:01:52.959 --> 01:01:54.718
and the phone was somewhere in the middle.

01:01:54.718 --> 01:01:59.718
As I was walking down this cliffside,

01:01:59.726 --> 01:02:01.861
I was thinking to myself,

01:02:01.861 --> 01:02:06.861
am I going to die trying
to find my kid's phone?

01:02:08.201 --> 01:02:10.775
He's never gonna forgive himself,

01:02:10.775 --> 01:02:14.340
is he gonna watch his dad

01:02:14.340 --> 01:02:16.547
tumble off this cliff in the moonlight

01:02:16.547 --> 01:02:21.547
and be like, ah, my dad's dead
because I dropped my phone.

01:02:21.623 --> 01:02:26.040
So that's when I started to
joke with them about that.

01:02:28.122 --> 01:02:29.640
Yeah, again, Lando.

01:02:29.640 --> 01:02:30.826
Lando didn't like that.

01:02:30.826 --> 01:02:31.659
Yeah.

01:02:31.659 --> 01:02:32.678
'Cause I was like, all right Shepherd,

01:02:32.678 --> 01:02:34.970
here I am, going to find your phone,

01:02:34.970 --> 01:02:36.993
this might be the last time we talk.

01:02:38.478 --> 01:02:39.388
You're twisted.

01:02:39.388 --> 01:02:40.398
I'm a little twisted.

01:02:40.398 --> 01:02:41.759
Scaring the crap out of children.

01:02:41.759 --> 01:02:45.453
But I also felt like I wasn't gonna die.

01:02:46.623 --> 01:02:49.550
Dismembering birds, who are you?

01:02:49.550 --> 01:02:51.010
That was my brother,

01:02:51.010 --> 01:02:53.096
and it wasn't like at the end of the cliff

01:02:53.096 --> 01:02:54.810
it just dropped off to nothingness,

01:02:54.810 --> 01:02:56.540
it was just the trail.

01:02:56.540 --> 01:02:57.660
Right.
So I was like,

01:02:57.660 --> 01:03:00.020
at worst, I'll just be badly injured.

01:03:00.020 --> 01:03:02.692
Right, you're not gonna
fall into oblivion,

01:03:02.692 --> 01:03:04.938
you're gonna fall to the
trail where I'm standing.

01:03:04.938 --> 01:03:07.305
But, as you know, I'm scared of heights,

01:03:07.305 --> 01:03:11.261
I was trying to overcome that
and not let anybody know.

01:03:11.261 --> 01:03:14.220
But eventually, I was like,
I can't see this phone

01:03:14.220 --> 01:03:15.053
and I'm afraid

01:03:15.053 --> 01:03:17.990
that if I get to a place
where I can't get back up,

01:03:17.990 --> 01:03:20.494
then we're gonna be
calling in the chopper,

01:03:20.494 --> 01:03:22.070
assuming there is one.

01:03:22.070 --> 01:03:25.046
And so, come back up, we
go around to the bottom

01:03:25.046 --> 01:03:26.383
and we get down there,

01:03:26.383 --> 01:03:30.223
and Shepherd, of course, is
already climbing up the cliff

01:03:30.223 --> 01:03:32.920
to try to get his phone.

01:03:32.920 --> 01:03:34.095
He might be addicted to screens,

01:03:34.095 --> 01:03:36.606
that might be what was driving him.

01:03:36.606 --> 01:03:38.759
He's way up there

01:03:38.759 --> 01:03:40.876
and I'm a little worried about him,

01:03:40.876 --> 01:03:43.958
and then I'm like, well,
I gotta go up there too.

01:03:43.958 --> 01:03:48.667
So I went up there and
five minutes passed,

01:03:48.667 --> 01:03:51.550
I ended up finding the phone,

01:03:51.550 --> 01:03:53.673
I was able to spot it with
my light and we got it.

01:03:53.673 --> 01:03:57.855
And we got out with only one,
maybe two curse words said,

01:03:57.855 --> 01:03:58.966
and no injuries.

01:03:58.966 --> 01:04:00.739
Yeah, Lando was cool with that,

01:04:00.739 --> 01:04:02.595
that's the end of that story.

01:04:02.595 --> 01:04:06.250
He still said, my favorite
thing was the night hike,

01:04:06.250 --> 01:04:08.202
so you didn't ruin it for him.

01:04:08.202 --> 01:04:09.421
Good.
Completely.

01:04:09.421 --> 01:04:11.836
But yeah, that's the catch phrase now,

01:04:11.836 --> 01:04:14.520
if anything wrong happens,

01:04:14.520 --> 01:04:15.353
first thing you need to say is,

01:04:15.353 --> 01:04:17.433
okay, please, nobody say any bad words.

01:04:20.179 --> 01:04:22.015
I'm about to tell you something
you're not gonna wanna hear,

01:04:22.015 --> 01:04:23.678
just don't say any bad words.

01:04:23.678 --> 01:04:26.142
It ended up being an incredible trip.

01:04:26.142 --> 01:04:30.179
I'm so glad, we're saying
that it was your idea,

01:04:30.179 --> 01:04:32.323
I'm so glad you had that idea.

01:04:33.602 --> 01:04:36.748
I wish we had had already done it more,

01:04:36.748 --> 01:04:40.123
now that our kids are
the age that they are.

01:04:42.360 --> 01:04:44.150
They'll wanna go next time.

01:04:44.150 --> 01:04:45.330
Here's the thing.
That's the good news.

01:04:45.330 --> 01:04:47.369
Is that before we went on that trip,

01:04:47.369 --> 01:04:49.336
I think, in Locke's mind it was like,

01:04:49.336 --> 01:04:52.368
I'm not gonna do this
again, but, by the end,

01:04:52.368 --> 01:04:55.728
he was talking about how
good of a time he had had,

01:04:55.728 --> 01:04:57.727
how much he liked being out in nature.

01:04:57.727 --> 01:05:02.699
And then, on the way back,
I drove Locke and Lincoln,

01:05:02.699 --> 01:05:07.394
and I let them be my DJ

01:05:07.394 --> 01:05:12.394
and, boy, the musical
journey that we went on

01:05:14.482 --> 01:05:19.482
that started in dad music
territory, John Mayer, et cetera,

01:05:20.879 --> 01:05:25.879
slowly started getting into
some hip hop that I enjoy,

01:05:29.277 --> 01:05:34.277
then began to transition into
maybe, some modern hip hop,

01:05:37.754 --> 01:05:39.913
à la 21 Savage.

01:05:42.284 --> 01:05:45.200
Talk about bad words.

01:05:45.200 --> 01:05:50.200
We made up for all the bad
words that we had held back

01:05:50.993 --> 01:05:52.363
in the presence of Lando,

01:05:52.363 --> 01:05:55.026
we went well over the limit.

01:05:55.026 --> 01:05:56.960
If you include what the
artists were saying.

01:05:56.960 --> 01:05:58.612
If you include what the artist said

01:05:58.612 --> 01:06:00.045
in the last hour of our trip.

01:06:00.045 --> 01:06:03.836
My van did not have the
capability to connect to my phone

01:06:03.836 --> 01:06:05.644
and most of the time.

01:06:05.644 --> 01:06:07.642
Oh, I was about to say that
Mercedes, but I forgot,

01:06:07.642 --> 01:06:09.010
no, you were in a Dodge.
I'm in a minivan

01:06:09.010 --> 01:06:09.843
at this point,

01:06:09.843 --> 01:06:12.273
it did not connect to my phone

01:06:12.273 --> 01:06:15.902
and we hardly had any radio stations,

01:06:15.902 --> 01:06:17.723
so the only thing I listened to

01:06:17.723 --> 01:06:21.582
was how bad my kid's and
your kid's feet smell.

01:06:21.582 --> 01:06:22.415
Yeah.

01:06:22.415 --> 01:06:24.493
Like, we had to pull over,

01:06:25.332 --> 01:06:27.694
Shepherd was wearing shoes with no socks.

01:06:27.694 --> 01:06:30.700
And he'll take his shoes
off when he gets in the car,

01:06:30.700 --> 01:06:32.049
can't let him do that.
I'm like, man,

01:06:32.049 --> 01:06:34.371
we had to pull over so he
could go up to your car,

01:06:34.371 --> 01:06:35.927
pull out that drawer,
and get some socks out.

01:06:35.927 --> 01:06:37.155
It's like a biohazard.

01:06:37.155 --> 01:06:39.360
We had a great trip,

01:06:39.360 --> 01:06:41.647
it's our privilege to
have shared it with you

01:06:41.647 --> 01:06:44.439
on this "Ear Biscuit", a great
way to kick off the year,

01:06:44.439 --> 01:06:49.172
it's just a good thing to build on,

01:06:49.172 --> 01:06:51.070
is that we can be outdoors.

01:06:51.070 --> 01:06:52.954
I still don't know what
I wanna do about the van,

01:06:52.954 --> 01:06:55.277
if there's a van in my future,

01:06:55.277 --> 01:06:57.856
I think I'm just gonna
stay on the rental front

01:06:57.856 --> 01:06:59.466
'cause this kind of
scared me a little bit,

01:06:59.466 --> 01:07:04.353
something that drastic, that
required that much towing.

01:07:06.989 --> 01:07:08.650
It was nice to rent it
and not be responsible.

01:07:08.650 --> 01:07:10.380
I think the rental.

01:07:10.380 --> 01:07:11.220
Is nice, yeah.

01:07:11.220 --> 01:07:12.315
Is the way to go for one of those.

01:07:12.315 --> 01:07:13.148
Yeah.

01:07:13.148 --> 01:07:13.981
It's not that hard to load your stuff up.

01:07:13.981 --> 01:07:15.973
That's what I'm leaning
towards at this point.

01:07:18.022 --> 01:07:20.061
It was a good trip, man.

01:07:20.061 --> 01:07:23.733
And next week we will talk about,

01:07:24.739 --> 01:07:28.094
